//! Runtime support for rust code generated by adlc, the compiler for the
//! [Algebraic Data Language](https://github.com/adl-lang/adl).
//!
//! You do not call this crate directly. adlc generates rust that refers to it,
//! and you depend on it so that the generated code compiles.
//!
//! It holds the few things generated code needs that the standard library does
//! not provide in an ADL compatible form:
//!
//! - [`ByteVector`], ADL's `Bytes`, serialized as base64.
//! - [`Map`](custom::sys::types::map::Map),
//!   [`Set`](custom::sys::types::set::Set),
//!   [`Maybe`](custom::sys::types::maybe::Maybe),
//!   [`Pair`](custom::sys::types::pair::Pair) and
//!   [`Result`](custom::sys::types::result::Result), the custom mappings for
//!   ADL's `sys.types` declarations. Each wraps the obvious standard library
//!   type and gives it ADL's json encoding, which is not the one serde would
//!   choose: ADL's `Maybe`, for instance, is `{"just": v}` or `"nothing"`,
//!   rather than `v` or `null`.
//! - [`AdlAnnotations`], re-exported from the companion `adl_rt_derive` crate.
//!
//! Paths under [`custom`] mirror the ADL module they implement, because that
//! is what adlc writes into the code it generates.
//!
//! The same module tree is available without this crate: adlc's `--include-rt`
//! flag copies `src/` into a generated project as a plain module, which is how
//! projects predating this crate obtain it. That is why the tree lives in
//! mod.rs, and why this file holds only what makes sense for a crate but not
//! for a vendored copy.

#[path = "mod.rs"]
mod rt;

pub use rt::*;

/// Re-exported so that generated code needs only this one dependency, in the
/// same way that serde::Serialize is a re-export of serde_derive::Serialize.
pub use adl_rt_derive::AdlAnnotations;
